Subject: RegExp: rarest-unit literal search, case-insensitive ASCII literals, anchored literal prefix, prefilter for first-set patterns

What this does
  - libregexp.c `lre_analyze`: an ASCII letter under /i outside Unicode mode is accepted into the required literal as a lower-case unit matched in either case (bit in `LRERequired.ci`); ASCII digits and punctuation under /i are exact units. The unit least likely to occur in text (`rare`) is chosen by a fixed score. For an anchored pattern the exact 8-bit units that follow the `^` are recorded (`apre`).
  - quickjs.c: the literal is searched by memchr (or an in-line scan for a case-insensitive unit) on its rare unit and then compared in full; an anchored pattern rejects on a mismatching prefix before entering the matcher; the first-set strategy also takes the required-literal reject and start adjustment.

Why we need it
  Post-0532 census: the literal-after-a-group pattern still cost 8,005 cycles per scan (a frequent first byte); `/TNQP=([^;]*)/i` (1,840 cycles, 24,480 scans, no hit) and `/%\w?$/` (2,341 cycles, 13,320 scans) ran the first-set scan over whole subjects; `/^ba/` entered the matcher for every call.

Soundness
  Under /i outside Unicode mode only the two ASCII cases of a letter canonicalize alike (non-ASCII never maps to ASCII); in Unicode mode letters are left out of the literal because U+212A and U+017F fold to k and s. Everything else is as in the required-literal analysis.

Failure modes
  The rarity score is a heuristic; a subject that is full of the chosen unit costs one comparison per occurrence.

  - The analysis entry point is renamed `lre_analyze2` because its result struct changed: an object compiled before this patch no longer links instead of silently filling a struct of another size (a stale libregexp.o gave "Wrong checksum" on the Octane regexp row).

